## 2. We need to show that

### Example
```coq
Lemma example_we_need_to_show_that :
  2 = 2.
Proof.
We need to show that 2 = 2.
We conclude that 2 = 2.
Qed.
```

> This restates the current goal, without proving anything.

### Try it yourself
```coq
Lemma exercise_we_need_to_show_that :
  7 = 7.
Proof.
```
<input-area>
```coq

```
</input-area>
```coq
Qed.
```

## 3. Proving "for every x, ...": take an arbitrary value

To prove a statement holds for every element of a set, introduce a fixed but unspecified element and prove the statement for it, without assuming anything extra about it.

### Example
```coq
Lemma example_take :
  ∀ x ∈ ℝ, ∀ y ∈ ℝ,
    x + y = y + x.
Proof.
Take x ∈ ℝ.
Take y ∈ ℝ.
We conclude that x + y = y + x.
Qed.
```

> Each variable in the statement needs its own `Take` line.

### Try it yourself
```coq
Lemma exercise_take :
  ∀ x ∈ ℝ,
    x + 3 = 3 + x.
Proof.
```
<input-area>
```coq

```
</input-area>
```coq
Qed.
```

## 4. Proving "there exists x such that ...": choose a value

To prove an existence statement, provide one specific value and show it satisfies the required property.

### Example
```coq
Lemma example_choose :
  ∃ y ∈ ℝ,
    y < 3.
Proof.
Choose y := 2.
{ Indeed, y ∈ ℝ. } (* Note: there needs to be a space between '.' and '}' *)
We conclude that y < 3.
Qed.
```

> The `{ Indeed, y ∈ ℝ. }` line confirms the chosen value belongs to the required set.

### Try it yourself
```coq
Lemma exercise_choose :
  ∃ z ∈ ℝ,
    z < 14.
Proof.
```
<input-area>
```coq

```
</input-area>
```coq
Qed.
```

## 5. Proving "if P, then Q": make an assumption

To prove a statement of the form "if P, then Q", assume P is true and show that Q follows.

### Example
```coq
Lemma example_assumptions :
  ∀ x ∈ ℤ,
    x > 0 ⇒ x + 1 > 1.
Proof.
Take x ∈ ℤ.
Assume that x > 0.
We conclude that x + 1 > 1.
Qed.
```

### Another example with explicit labels
```coq
Lemma example_assumptions_2 :
  ∀ x ∈ ℤ,
    x > 0 ⇒ x + 1 > 1.
Proof.
Take x ∈ ℤ.
Assume that x > 0 as (i). (* The label here is optional *)
By (i) we conclude that x + 1 > 1.
Qed.
```

> Labelling an assumption with `as (i)` allows it to be referenced later using `By (i) ...`.

### Try it yourself
```coq
Lemma exercise_assumptions :
  ∀ a ∈ ℤ,
    a ≥ 2 ⇒ a + 3 > 4.
Proof.
```
<input-area>
```coq

```
</input-area>
```coq
Qed.
```

## 6. Proving "P and Q": prove two statements

To prove a conjunction, prove each part separately.

### Example
```coq
Lemma example_both_statements :
  ∀ x ∈ ℝ, x^2 ≥ 0 ∧ | x | ≥ 0.
Proof.
Take x ∈ ℝ.
We show both statements.
- We need to show that x^2 ≥ 0.
  We conclude that x^2 ≥ 0.
- We need to show that | x | ≥ 0.
  We conclude that | x | ≥ 0.
Qed.
```

> Each `-` bullet is a complete, independent proof of one part.

### Try it yourself
```coq
Lemma exercise_both_statements :
  ∀ x ∈ ℝ, 0 * x = 0 ∧ x + 1 > x.
Proof.
```
<input-area>
```coq

```
</input-area>
```coq
Qed.
```

## 7. Proving "P if and only if Q": show both directions

A statement of the form "P if and only if Q" requires two separate proofs: "if P then Q" and "if Q then P".

### Example
```coq
Lemma example_both_directions :
  ∀ x ∈ ℝ, ∀ y ∈ ℝ,
    x < y ⇔ y > x.
Proof.
Take x ∈ ℝ.
Take y ∈ ℝ.
We show both directions.
- We need to show that x < y ⇒ y > x.
  Assume that x < y.
  We conclude that y > x.
- We need to show that y > x ⇒ x < y.
  Assume that y > x.
  We conclude that x < y.
Qed.
```

> Each `-` bullet proves one direction independently, using the same assume-and-conclude pattern as Section 5.

### Try it yourself
```coq
Lemma exercise_both_directions :
  ∀ x ∈ ℝ, x > 1 ⇔ x - 1 > 0.
Proof.
```
<input-area>
```coq

```
</input-area>
```coq
Qed.
```

## 8. Proving "P or Q": prove one of them

To prove "P or Q", it is enough to prove just one of P or Q. Pick the one that is true and prove it.

### Example
```coq
Lemma example_or :
  ∀ x ∈ ℝ,
    x > 5 ⇒ (x > 2 ∨ x < 0).
Proof.
Take x ∈ ℝ.
Assume that x > 5.
It suffices to show that x > 2.
We conclude that x > 2.
Qed.
```

> `It suffices to show that x > 2.` picks the side of the "or" to prove.

### Try it yourself
```coq
Lemma exercise_or :
  ∀ x ∈ ℝ,
    x < -3 ⇒ (x > 10 ∨ x < 0).
Proof.
```
<input-area>
```coq

```
</input-area>
```coq
Qed.
```

## 9. Combining several of the above in one proof

Statements that mix quantifiers and implications are handled by addressing each part in the order it appears.

### Example
```coq
Lemma example_combine_quantifiers :
  ∀ a ∈ ℝ, ∀ b ∈ ℝ,
    b > 5 ⇒ ∃ c ∈ ℝ, c > b - a.
Proof.
Take a ∈ ℝ.
Take b ∈ ℝ.
Assume that b > 5.
Choose c := b - a + 1.
{ Indeed, c ∈ ℝ. }
We conclude that c > b - a.
Qed.
```

### Try it yourself
```coq
Lemma exercise_combine_quantifiers :
  ∀ x ∈ ℝ, ∀ y ∈ ℝ,
    x > 3 ⇒ ∃ z ∈ ℝ, x < z - y.
Proof.
```
<input-area>
```coq

```
</input-area>
```coq
Qed.
```

## 10. Expanding definitions

Statements that use a custom definition, rather than a built-in operator, sometimes need that definition unfolded before it can be reasoned about directly.

### Example

Here is the definition of the square function, as an example.
```coq
Definition square (x : ℝ) := x^2.
```
<hint title="🔧 Technical details (click to open/close)">
```coq
Waterproof Register Expand "square";
  for square;
  as "Definition square".
```
</hint>

By writing `Expand square.` you get suggestions on how to use the definition of _square_ in your proof.
Alternatively, you can just write `Expand All.` and you get suggestions for all definitions that
Waterproof knows about.

After writing one of these, you can click on the button `replace` to replace the line
`Expand square.` or `Expand All.` with one of the suggested statements.

```coq
Lemma example_expand :
  ∀ x ∈ ℝ, square x ≥ 0.
Proof.
Take x ∈ ℝ.
Expand square.
  (* Remove the above line in your own code! *)
Expand All.
  (* Remove the above line in your own code! *)
We need to show that x^2 ≥ 0.
We conclude that x^2 ≥ 0.
Qed.
```

> `Expand square.` reveals that `square x` means `x^2`, allowing the goal to be restated in terms already known to the automation.

### Try it yourself
```coq
Lemma exercise_expand :
  ∀ x ∈ ℝ, - (square x) ≤ 0.
Proof.
```
<input-area>
```coq

```
</input-area>
```coq
Qed.
```

## 11. Using a fact that is an "and"

If a hypothesis has the form "P and Q", it can be split into its two parts to be used separately.

### Example
```coq
Lemma example_use_conjunction :
  ∀ x ∈ ℝ, ∀ y ∈ ℝ,
    (x > 0 ∧ y > 0) ⇒ x + y > 0.
Proof.
Take x ∈ ℝ.
Take y ∈ ℝ.
Assume that x > 0 ∧ y > 0.
It holds that x > 0.
It holds that y > 0.
We conclude that x + y > 0.
Qed.
```

> `It holds that x > 0.` records one part of the "and" as a separate fact. `It holds that` is covered in more detail in a later section on "working forwards".

### Try it yourself
```coq
Lemma exercise_use_conjunction :
  ∀ x ∈ ℝ, ∀ y ∈ ℝ,
    (x > 1 ∧ y > 1) ⇒ x * y > 1.
Proof.
```
<input-area>
```coq

```
</input-area>
```coq
Qed.
```

## 12. Using a fact that is existentially quantified

If a hypothesis states that some value exists with a given property, that value can be named and its property used directly.

### Example
```coq
Lemma example_use_there_exists :
  ∀ x ∈ ℝ,
    (∃ y ∈ ℝ, y > 10 ∧ y < x) ⇒
      10 < x.
Proof.
Take x ∈ ℝ.
Assume that ∃ y ∈ ℝ, y > 10 ∧ y < x.
Obtain such a y.
It holds that y > 10.
It holds that y < x.
We conclude that 10 < x.
Qed.
```

### Another example
```coq
Lemma example_use_there_exists_2 :
  ∀ x ∈ ℝ,
    (∃ y ∈ ℝ, y > 14 ∧ y < x) ⇒
      12 < x.
Proof.
Take x ∈ ℝ.
Assume that ∃ y ∈ ℝ, y > 14 ∧ y < x as (i).
Obtain y according to (i).
It holds that y > 14.
It holds that y < x.
We conclude that 12 < x.
Qed.
```

> `Obtain such a y.` (or `Obtain y according to (i).`) introduces the value named in the existence statement as a usable variable, with its stated properties attached.

### Try it yourself
```coq
Lemma exercise_use_there_exists :
  ∀ z ∈ ℝ,
    (∃ x ∈ ℝ, x ≥ 5 ∧ x^2 < z) ⇒
      25 < z.
Proof.
```
<input-area>
```coq

```
</input-area>
```coq
Qed.
```

## 13. Using a fact that is an "or"

If a hypothesis has the form "P or Q", we don't know which of the two is true, so the proof is split into cases: one assuming P, and one assuming Q. The goal must be proved in both cases.

### Example
```coq
Lemma example_use_disjunction :
  ∀ x ∈ ℝ,
    (x = 2 ∨ x = 3) ⇒ x > 1.
Proof.
Take x ∈ ℝ.
Assume that x = 2 ∨ x = 3 as (i).
Because (i) either x = 2 or x = 3.
- Case x = 2.
  We conclude that x > 1.
- Case x = 3.
  We conclude that x > 1.
Qed.
```

> `Because (i) either ... or ...` splits the "or" in hypothesis (i) into two cases. Each `-` bullet proves the goal under one of them.

### Another example: splitting into cases without an "or" hypothesis

You can also split into cases yourself, as long as the cases cover every possibility. This is useful when proving an "or" where which side is true depends on the situation.

```coq
Lemma example_cases :
  ∀ x ∈ ℝ, ∀ y ∈ ℝ,
    max(x, y) = x ∨ max(x, y) = y.
Proof.
Take x ∈ ℝ.
Take y ∈ ℝ.
Either x < y or x ≥ y.
- Case x < y.
  It suffices to show that max(x, y) = y.
  We conclude that max(x, y) = y.
- Case x ≥ y.
  It suffices to show that max(x, y) = x.
  We conclude that max(x, y) = x.
Qed.
```

> `Either ... or ...` states the two possibilities that cover every case. Each `-` bullet proves the goal under one of them.

### Try it yourself
```coq
Lemma exercise_use_disjunction :
  ∀ x ∈ ℝ, ∀ y ∈ ℝ,
    (x > y ∨ x = y) ⇒ x ≥ y.
Proof.
```
<input-area>
```coq

```
</input-area>
```coq
Qed.
```

### Try it yourself (splitting into cases)
```coq
Lemma exercise_cases :
  ∀ x ∈ ℝ, ∀ y ∈ ℝ,
    min(x, y) = x ∨ min(x, y) = y.
Proof.
```
<input-area>
```coq

```
</input-area>
```coq
Qed.
```

## 14. Working forwards: "It holds that"

New facts can be derived from what is already known, one step at a time, until the goal is reached.

### Example
```coq
Lemma example_forwards :
  ∀ x ∈ ℝ,
    x > 7 ⇒ x > 2.
Proof.
Take x ∈ ℝ.
Assume that x > 7.
It holds that x > 3.
We conclude that x > 2.
Qed.
```

> To derive a fact from a specific named hypothesis or lemma, write `By (h) it holds that ...`.

### Another example: using an assumed implication

```coq
Lemma example_forwards_implication :
  ∀ x ∈ ℝ,
    ((x > 0 ⇒ x + 1 > 1) ∧ x > 5) ⇒ x + 1 > 1.
Proof.
Take x ∈ ℝ.
Assume that (x > 0 ⇒ x + 1 > 1) ∧ x > 5.
It holds that x > 0 ⇒ x + 1 > 1 as (i).
It holds that x > 5.
It holds that x > 0.
By (i) it holds that x + 1 > 1.
We conclude that x + 1 > 1.
Qed.
```

> This can be used to derive the conclusion of an implication if we have already established the hypothesis.

### Try it yourself
```coq
Lemma exercise_forwards :
  ∀ x ∈ ℝ,
    x > 8 ⇒ x ≥ 3.
Proof.
```
<input-area>
```coq

```
</input-area>
```coq
Qed.
```

## 15. Using a fact that is universally quantified

If a hypothesis holds for every element of a set, it can be applied to one specific value of interest.

### Example
```coq
Lemma example_use_for_all :
  ∀ x ∈ ℝ,
    (∀ ε ∈ ℝ, ε > 0 ⇒ x < ε) ⇒
       x + 1 / 2 < 1.
Proof.
Take x ∈ ℝ.
Assume that ∀ ε ∈ ℝ, ε > 0 ⇒ x < ε as (i).
Use ε := 1 / 2 in (i).
{ Indeed, 1 / 2 ∈ ℝ. }
It holds that 1 / 2 > 0 ⇒ x < 1 / 2.
It holds that 1 / 2 > 0.
It holds that x < 1 / 2.
We conclude that x + 1 / 2 < 1.
Qed.
```

> `Use ε := 1 / 2 in (i).` applies hypothesis (i) to the specific value 1 / 2. Any remaining conditions from (i), such as `1 / 2 > 0`, still need to be justified before the resulting fact can be used.

### Try it yourself
```coq
Lemma exercise_use_for_all:
  ∀ x ∈ ℝ,
    (∀ ε ∈ ℝ, ε > 0 ⇒ x < ε) ⇒
       10 * x < 1.
Proof.
```
<input-area>
```coq

```
</input-area>
```coq
Qed.
```

## 16. Working backwards: "It suffices to show that"

If proving a simpler statement would automatically make the goal true, the goal can be replaced with that simpler statement.

### Example
```coq
Lemma example_backwards :
  ∀ x ∈ ℝ,
    x > 3 ⇒ x + 2 > 4.
Proof.
Take x ∈ ℝ.
Assume that x > 3.
It suffices to show that x > 2.
We conclude that x > 2.
Qed.
```

### Try it yourself
```coq
Lemma exercise_backwards :
  ∀ x ∈ ℝ,
    x > 5 ⇒ x - 1 > 3.
Proof.
```
<input-area>
```coq

```
</input-area>
```coq
Qed.
```

## 17. Chains of (in)equalities

Several connected inequalities can be written as a single chain instead of separate steps.

### Example
```coq
Lemma example_inequalities :
  ∀ a ∈ ℝ, ∀ b ∈ ℝ, ∀ c ∈ ℝ,
    (a < b ∧ b < c) ⇒ a < c.
Proof.
Take a ∈ ℝ.
Take b ∈ ℝ.
Take c ∈ ℝ.
Assume that a < b ∧ b < c.
It holds that a < b.
It holds that b < c.
We conclude that & a < b < c.
Qed.
```

> The `&` chain is checked one link at a time (`a < b`, then `b < c`) before the overall relation (`a < c`) is accepted.

### Try it yourself
```coq
Lemma exercise_inequalities:
  ∀ x ∈ ℝ, ∀ y ∈ ℝ,
    (x < y ∧ y < x + 5) ⇒ x < x + 5.
Proof.
```
<input-area>
```coq

```
</input-area>
```coq
Qed.
```

## 18. Arguing by contradiction

To prove a statement, assume it is false and show that this leads to a contradiction.

### Example
```coq
Lemma example_contradiction :
  ∀ x ∈ ℝ,
    (∀ ε ∈ ℝ, ε > 0 ⇒ x > 1 - ε) ⇒
      x ≥ 1.
Proof.
Take x ∈ ℝ.
Assume that ∀ ε ∈ ℝ, ε > 0 ⇒ x > 1 - ε as (i).
We need to show that x ≥ 1.
We argue by contradiction.
Assume that ¬ (x ≥ 1).
It holds that (1 - x) > 0.
By (i) it holds that x > 1 - (1 - x).
It holds that x > x.
Contradiction.
Qed.
```

> `Contradiction.` finishes the proof once you have derived something impossible, like `x > x`.

### Try it yourself
```coq
Lemma exercise_contradiction :
  ∀ x ∈ ℝ,
    (∀ ε ∈ ℝ, ε > 0 ⇒ x < ε)
      ⇒ x ≤ 0.
Proof.
```
<input-area>
```coq

```
</input-area>
```coq
Qed.
```

## 19. Proof by induction

To prove a statement for every natural number, show it holds for 0 (the base case), then show that if it holds for some number k, it also holds for k + 1 (the inductive step).

<hint title="Notations setup (click to open/close)">
Since this section works with natural numbers instead of real numbers, we first switch the default scope so that `<`, `≤`, `+`, etc. are read as operations on ℕ:
```coq
Close Scope R_scope.
Open Scope nat_scope.
```
</hint>

### Example
```coq
Lemma example_induction :
  ∀ n : ℕ → ℕ, (∀ k ∈ ℕ, n(k) < n(k + 1)) ⇒
    ∀ k ∈ ℕ, k ≤ n(k).
Proof.
Take n : ℕ → ℕ.
Assume that ∀ k ∈ ℕ, n(k) < n(k + 1).
We use induction on k.
- We first show the base case 0 ≤ n(0).
  We conclude that 0 ≤ n(0).
- We now show the induction step.
  Take k ∈ ℕ.
  Assume that k ≤ n(k).
  It holds that n(k) < n(k + 1).
  It holds that n(k) + 1 ≤ n(k + 1).
  We conclude that & k + 1 ≤ n(k) + 1 ≤ n(k + 1).
Qed.
```

> The first `-` bullet is the base case. The second is the inductive step: `Take k ∈ ℕ.` introduces an arbitrary number, `Assume that k ≤ n(k).` is the induction hypothesis, and the rest of the proof shows the statement then holds for the next number. Waterproof writes k + 1 as `S(k)` inside the induction step.

### Try it yourself
```coq
Lemma exercise_induction :
  ∀ F : ℕ → ℕ, (∀ k ∈ ℕ, F(k + 1) = F(k)) ⇒
    ∀ k ∈ ℕ, F(k) = F(0).
Proof.
```
<input-area>
```coq

```
</input-area>
```coq
Qed.
```
